  {
    "episode_id": 83881,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/3/83881.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 85,
    "turn_text": "George Washington's father, he actually passed away when George was just 11 years old, and so his mom, Mary Ball Washington, she raised six kids on her own, although one did die in infancy, but she actually played a really big role in George's life. George Washington, he wanted to be a cabin boy in the British Navy, and his mom convinced him that that was a bad idea. She wrote to her half-brother John, who lived in London, And he wrote back and said, do not allow George to enter the British Navy because they treated colonists so poorly. And there was even a risk that he would get sick on one of those ships and die and wouldn't be taken care of properly. And George was really open about the influence that his mother had on him. She was an incredibly strong woman of faith. And she helped George to cultivate that in himself to have his own firm foundation and faith. So pretty neat to see. She played such a critical role in raising up George Washington, who became our first president.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_03",
